virglrenderer: a separable control shader writes its padding patch varyings on the Mac (#589)

What: between two separable stages on desktop GL, virgl-shader-separable-patch-varyings.patch
has the control shader declare every patch varying from 0 up to its highest one (Apple's GL
matches separable patch varyings by the order of their names). The padding ones were
declared and never written. Now the control shader also writes each of them at the start of
main ("patch00 = vec4(0.0);"), so every way out of main has written them. The evaluation
shader, whole programs, GLES and control shaders without a gap keep their GLSL; no key bit,
no new shader variant (it uses that patch's separable_out).
Why: Apple's GL links and draws such a pipeline right, but its Metal pipeline build fails
for a control shader with a patch output it never writes ("Fallback to SW vertex processing
because buildPipelineState failed"): on the M1 Max (macOS 27) a separable pipeline with
patch varyings 1 and 3 (or only 1) ran on Apple's CPU vertex path, its first draw took
250-370 ms instead of 5-120 ms, and Metal's shader compiler (MTLCompilerService) sometimes
crashed. With the padding written: no fallback, the same pixels. Apple's software renderer
never had the failure.
Upstream: not sent (a workaround for Apple's GL, on top of the patch-varyings patch).
Test: Tests/virgl/test-core-glsl-shaders.c: a separable control shader with patch varyings
1 and 3, only 1, a patch array at 2..3 with an indirect index, and a barrier and a return
writes exactly the padding it declares, each declared once, and compiles on the Mac's GL;
with patch varyings 0 to 11, before a whole evaluation shader, as a whole program, on GLES
and in the evaluation shader nothing is written. Tests/virgl/test-separable-pervertex.c D4
and D7 draw the same on Apple's software renderer (the GPU fallback is the M1 rows in the
PR).

